#### 3. Human Analytical Narrative — The 'Why' Factor

**Chart 1 reading:** The data confirms Davao Region's tourism sector experienced a near-total collapse in 2020 — domestic arrivals fell from **2.1 million (2019) to 420,000**, and foreign arrivals from **95,000 to 8,500**. Revenue dropped from ₱22.5 billion to ₱4.1 billion. Recovery was not linear: 2021 showed only partial rebound before the sector surged in 2022–2024, reaching **4.1 million total arrivals and ₱34.7 billion** in revenue by 2024. What the AI summarizer did not flag — but human review of DOT-11 statements confirms — is that this recovery was **domestically led first**. Foreign arrivals only recovered meaningfully in 2023 (103,474) and accelerated sharply in 2024 (230,000). The AI's default framing of tourism recovery through a foreign-arrivals lens, as seen in the Literature Verification exercise, would have misread this chart entirely.

**Chart 2 reading:** The IGACOS visitor dip in 2022 (218,000 — down from 345,000 pre-pandemic despite overall regional recovery) is not a market failure. Cross-referencing with Dalagan & Sy Jr. (2023) confirms this gap reflects a **deliberate resort reconstruction phase**: operators used pandemic closures to renovate facilities and reduce operating capacity before reopening. By 2024, IGACOS rebounded to a record **420,000 visitors** with 74 operational resorts — the highest count in the dataset. The Mt. Apo trekker line tells a structurally different story: **it has not recovered to its 2019 peak (15,200)** and likely never will — by institutional design. The PAMB 50-trekker-per-trail-per-day cap, confirmed in the Literature Review, deliberately suppresses volume to protect highland biodiversity. The 2022 dip to 8,900 marks the cap's full enforcement. This divergence — IGACOS recovering toward volume, Mt. Apo managed toward restraint — represents the **core tension in the Davao Region's eco-tourism policy architecture**: coastal mass tourism versus highland conservation-first governance.

**Policy Implication for LGU Department Heads:** Budget allocation for tourism infrastructure should not treat IGACOS and MANP as equivalent destinations requiring equivalent development investment. IGACOS warrants investment in **carrying capacity expansion and marine waste management** to absorb volume growth sustainably. MANP requires investment in **ranger capacity, trail monitoring systems, and alternative livelihood programs** for surrounding communities — not expanded access infrastructure.

